{"chunk_id": "29224926f215", "text": "Bhishma recounts to Yudhishthira two instructive dialogues illustrating philosophical equanimity. First, the fallen Asura Namuchi explains to Indra why he does not grieve despite losing power and freedom: sorrow is useless, destiny is governed by a single Ordainer, and wise people remain steady amid pleasure and pain. The narrative then shifts to the defeated Daitya king Vali, whom Indra finds bound and stripped of his former sovereignty. When Indra questions his composure, Vali delivers a long reflection on Time (Kala) as the true ruler of the universe. Victories, defeats, prosperity, and ruin all arise through Time rather than personal merit. Countless mighty rulers before Indra have risen and vanished. Because all power is temporary, the wise neither boast in success nor despair in loss. The episode teaches stoic endurance, humility before cosmic order, and the impermanence of worldly authority.\nIndra questions fallen Asuras Namuchi and Vali, who calmly explain that Time and destiny rule all fortunes, making grief or pride foolish.\nThemes: inevitability of Time, detachment from fortune, fate vs agency, humility after power, stoic wisdom", "vector": [0.01974557153880596, -0.02780275046825409, 0.017991626635193825, -0.005265257321298122, -0.01240778248757124, 0.02902228944003582, -0.024075619876384735, -0.01962224766612053, -0.014579658396542072, -0.007139099761843681, 0.015840305015444756, -0.006803383585065603, 0.03455817326903343, 0.013236794620752335, -0.014195983298122883, 0.007707761134952307, 0.055084798485040665, 0.00021485383331310004, 0.031077692285180092, -0.008749165572226048, 0.0015886551700532436, 0.013826010748744011, 0.002055402845144272, 0.0024613449349999428, -0.016319898888468742, 0.027405371889472008, 0.03266720473766327, -0.01312717329710722, 0.01779978908598423, -0.03184504434466362, 0.007378896698355675, 0.00873546302318573, 0.0012615035520866513, -0.00933838076889515, -0.02348640374839306, -0.004309494